1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Confidentiality: an eavesdropper who copies every packet learns nothing about the message. Encryption is the only tool for this one.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Integrity: a changed message is detected. Authentication: the receiver knows who sent it, and the sender cannot later deny it.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Every question on this page is one of those three, and the first step is always to name which. The name decides the keys before any arithmetic starts.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Symmetric encryption uses one key for both directions: the same key locks and unlocks. DES and AES are the standard examples.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:50,000
If every pair in a group of N must have its own secret, the number of keys is N(N-1)/2: the number of pairs, not the number of people.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:00,000
That is the answer to GATE CSE 2015 Set 1: N(N-1)/2. The distractors are the per-person count N(N-1) and the two-keys-per-person count 2N.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Public-key encryption gives each person a pair: a public key anyone may read, and a private key only its owner can use.

8
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:20,000
For secrecy the sender encrypts with the receiver public key, and only the receiver private key opens it. Sender keys play no part.

9
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,000
For proof the direction reverses: the sender encrypts with their own private key, and anyone verifies with the sender public key.

10
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
RSA picks two primes p and q, sets n = pq, and computes phi(n) = (p-1)(q-1). With p = 13 and q = 17: n = 221 and phi(n) = 192.

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The public exponent e must share no factor with phi(n). The private exponent d is the inverse of e modulo phi(n): here 35 times 11 = 385 = 2 times 192 + 1.

12
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:00,000
GATE CSE 2019 gave n = 3007 and phi(n) = 2880. Then p + q = n - phi(n) + 1 = 128, the two primes are 31 and 97, and the factor above 50 is 97.

13
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Encryption is one modular power: the ciphertext is m^e mod n, and decryption is c^d mod n. Nothing else in RSA is arithmetic.

14
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Square-and-multiply reads the exponent in binary. For 51 = 110011 that is five squarings and four multiplications: nine in all, against fifty for the naive loop.

15
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:30,000
3^51 mod 5 = 2, computed both by square-and-multiply and by the cycle of powers mod 5. GATE CSE 2019 asked this, and it is one line of arithmetic.

16
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:40,000
A signature is not the message encrypted. The sender hashes the message and signs the digest, so the signature is short and fixed in size.

17
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:50,000
The transmitted pair is the message and the signed digest. GATE CSE 2016 Set 1 asked for exactly this form, and the answer used the sender private key.

18
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Verification uses the sender public key: that is the 2016 Set 2 answer, Anarkali public key. Under any other key the check fails, which the page demonstrates numerically.

19
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Signing gives proof. Encrypting gives secrecy. A message that needs both is signed first and encrypted second.

20
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:20,000
At X: apply X private key, then Y public key. At Y: apply Y private key, then X public key. That is GATE CSE 2013, and the answer was the last option.

21
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Reversing the order still works arithmetically, but the resulting arrangement is weaker: the signature is exposed, and the receiver may act on unverified data.

22
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:40,000
A hash maps any input to a fixed-length digest, one way. MD5 and SHA-1 are the two named in GATE CSE 2014, where RSA and DES were the distractors.

23
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Collisions arrive early: with 365 possible values, 23 items already collide with probability 0.507297, and 366 items must collide by pigeonhole.

24
00:03:50,000 --> 00:04:00,000
For a 64-bit digest a collision needs about 2^32 attempts, not 2^64. Only the signer can use that: a receiver would need a second preimage, at 2^64.

25
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:10,000
A certificate is a name, a public key, and a validity period, signed by a certificate authority that a stranger already trusts.

26
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Verification walks the chain: the browser trusts a root, the root signed the server certificate, so the server key is believed to belong to the server name.

27
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:30,000
A MAC is a shared-key check: it detects change and proves the sender to a fellow key holder, but it cannot be shown to a third party.

28
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:40,000
A packet filter reads the rule table top to bottom and stops at the first match. The page evaluates six rules against four packets and gets every decision by first match.

29
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:50,000
What a device can filter is what its layer can read: addresses at layer 3, ports and flags at layer 4, the actual request only at layer 7.

30
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:00,000
So a layer-4 firewall CANNOT block one user on a multi-user host: all of them share an address and use any port. GATE CSE 2011, answer D.

